@node WhatsNew "What's new in 1.2.0"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}MintVID 1.2.0@{ub}

This is a performance release for real accelerated m68k systems. The WMV7/8
and HLS stability work from 1.1.1 and the wider 1.1.0 improvements remain.

  - Speeds up H.264 inverse-transform/reconstruction by inlining the tiny
    operations previously called for individual coefficients and pixels.
  - Selects an exact branchless luma-deblocking primitive for MintVID060;
    MintVID030 and MintVID040 retain their established shorter sequence.
  - Enables MintAMP's CPU-aware AAC Huffman, dequantisation, stereo and IMDCT
    helpers by default. The 68030/040 editions use hardware full-result
    multiplication. MintVID060 uses bit-exact partial products and avoids the
    software-emulated register-pair MULS.L instruction.
  - Gives H.263, MPEG-2, MPEG-4 Part 2, MP42/DIV2, WMV1 and WMV2 the existing
    shared table-driven/m68k-ASM YUV output path.
  - Adds a million-pair 68060 AAC arithmetic check to CI alongside the host
    and big-endian m68k codec conformance suites.

Real-A1200 68060 testing confirmed clean stereo AAC and brought the lowest-
resolution BBC One H.264/HLS stream close to real time using AGA/HAM8. Turbo+
keeps audio continuous and displays keyframes when full video remains beyond
the machine. Performance still depends on CPU clock, stream and display mode.

See CHANGELOG.txt in the release archive, or CHANGELOG.md in the source tree,
for the detailed list.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node Overview "Overview"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}What MintVID is@{ub}

MintVID plays video files, YouTube, and IPTV channels directly on the
Amiga. It is built in the spirit of MintAMP (the libhelix-based audio
player): a small portable C core with thin Amiga-specific display and
audio layers around it. MP3/AAC decoding reuses the proven MintAMP/Helix
code, with audio output through Paula.

The goal is to go beyond MPEG-1 on real accelerated Amiga hardware.
MintVID provides a broad range of codecs, but codec support does not imply
real-time playback on every supported CPU. Practical performance depends
on processor speed, codec complexity, resolution, bitrate and display mode:

  - 68030-class ECS/AGA systems - best suited to lightweight codecs and
    modest frame sizes. Cinepak is the natural starting point; heavier
    formats may decode correctly without being practical in real time.
  - 68040/060 systems - older codecs such as Cinepak, MJPEG, MPEG-1/2,
    MPEG-4 Part 2 and WMV7/8 become more practical at modest resolutions,
    especially with RTG. H.264/AVC remains extremely demanding, but 1.2.0
    brought the lowest-resolution BBC One HLS stream close to real time on a
    tested real 68060 using AGA/HAM8. Higher resolutions remain beyond normal
    classic hardware, and results depend on the exact stream and configuration.
  - PiStorm/Emu68 (ARM-hosted 68k) - use the MintVID040 build. H.264/AVC
    (Baseline through High Profile, CABAC and B-frames) becomes much more
    practical. On the tested Pi3-based PiStorm 600, low-resolution H.264
    streams below roughly 200p have played well. Faster PiStorm hardware
    should provide more headroom, but exact results depend on the stream
    and configuration.
  - Vampire/Apollo 68080 - not yet validated by the MintVID project. No
    optimised build is officially recommended yet, and the MintVID060 build
    should not be assumed to be correct solely from the CPU name.

MintVID uses the same feature set across its 68030, 68040 and 68060 builds.
For normal classic systems use MintVID030, MintVID040 or MintVID060 for the
matching CPU. PiStorm/Emu68 uses MintVID040. There is no separate AGA, RTG or
PiStorm feature edition; display mode and practical playback performance
depend on the hardware underneath it.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node Installation "Installation"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}Installing MintVID@{ub}

For a normal 68030, 68040 or 68060 system use the MintVID030, MintVID040 or
MintVID060 drawer respectively. PiStorm/Emu68 users should use MintVID040.
Vampire/Apollo 68080 has not yet been validated, so there is no official
optimised-build recommendation for it yet. Copy the chosen drawer to your
Amiga (or a shared PiStorm volume) and keep its contents together.

Run @{b}MintVID@{ub} on systems with ReAction V44. A standard AmigaOS 3.0
system without ReAction V44 should instead run @{b}MintVID-GT@{ub}, the plain
GadTools/Intuition equivalent; it opens the matching -GT browsers
automatically. IPTV and YouTube browsing are reachable from the controller's
menus.

Everything else in the drawer (mrplay, iptvgui, ytgui, the GadTools -GT set,
mr_decode) is a support binary MintVID or the -GT controller loads on demand.
Nothing in the release drawer is intended to be split up or copied out on its
own.

@{b}Requirements@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 3.0 or later.
  - 68030 or better.
  - AmiSSL v5 + amisslmaster.library for HTTPS, YouTube and online IPTV
    features when the build was compiled with SSL=1.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node Controls "Controls"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}Playing a video@{ub}

Run the controller, choose a movie, and select a display mode: AGA,
HAM6, HAM8, or CGX. Laced and 2x apply to the three chipset modes; CGX
playback opens a size-gadget window and scales the video as that window
is resized. The C2P chooser selects the standard graphics.library path,
CD32 Akiko hardware, or the Kalms converter for chipset playback (it is
disabled for CGX). Kalms is the default; incompatible geometry or bitmap
layouts fall back safely to graphics.library.

On a HAM6 or HAM8 screen whose height is an exact whole fraction of the
decoded height (the 640x360 into 640x180 shape a non-laced fit normally
produces), H.264 is converted straight from YUV to HAM pixel bytes rather
than through a full-resolution RGB24 frame - only the rows the downscale
keeps are converted, cutting that conversion by about 45%. Other HAM
geometries keep the established route.

  Play          starts the selected movie
  Pause         toggles playback
  Stop          exits the player
  Fast forward  toggles unpaced decode

@{b}H.264 performance modes@{ub}

TurboGT remains the default. Auto resolves to the same policy.

  Quality   full filtering and no deliberate frame skipping
  Balanced  in-loop deblocking disabled; motion compensation stays exact
  Fast      Balanced plus bilinear instead of six-tap interpolation; still
            keeps every frame
  Turbo     Fast policy plus B-frame skipping; keeps the P-frame chain
  Turbo+    skips both P- and B-frames, so only keyframes are decoded and
            shown, keeping that decode short enough to hold audio through it;
            last-resort keyframe/slideshow mode
  TurboGT   same policy as Turbo, kept as a selectable name

Use Fast when keeping every frame matters more than maximum speed. Turbo and
TurboGT are now the same setting: what used to separate them was
disabling deblocking on keyframes as well, and every mode from Balanced down
now does that, because leaving some pictures undegraded corrupted the
decoder's per-macroblock deblocking state (it made Fast slower than Quality).
All of these modes are substantially faster than they were in 1.2.0.

@{b}Fullscreen (RTG/CGX only)@{ub}

Press @{b}F@{ub} to switch the live player between its resizeable window
and a borderless public-screen-sized view, without restarting decoding;
@{i}--fullscreen@{ui} starts in that view directly. Press F again - or use
ytgui's Fullscreen button - to restore the previous window geometry. AGA
display modes remain hotkey-driven and ignore the RTG-only fullscreen
command.

@{b}Seeking@{ub}

Cursor left/right seek 10 seconds at a time for local QuickTime MOV/MP4
files, landing on the nearest keyframe via the sample index - a real
seek, not a fast-decode approximation. AVI, MKV, and network/live
sources don't have a keyframe index yet, so cursor-right stays the
fast-forward toggle for those instead. Cursor up/down adjust Paula's
volume, the same 8/64 steps as ytgui's Vol -/Vol + buttons.

@{b}Playhead@{ub}

Once playback starts, the controller's Info: field mirrors a live
H:MM:SS/M:SS playhead in the current stream's own timeline, refreshed
about once a second, for local files and any stream played through the
IPTV/YouTube browsers - so a seek shows where it actually landed. On RTG
(CGX/P96), the same playhead also appears in the video window's own
title bar, visible even with the controller window elsewhere.

@{b}Every GUI@{ub} has a MintVID > About MintVID... menu with the project
credits and support link, and MintVID > Quit to close that frontend
cleanly. The controller's file gadget identifies the selected file, and
on launch mrplay reports the container type, video codec/FourCC,
dimensions, frame rate and audio format to its console.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node GUIEditions "The GUI editions"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}Two Workbench-friendly GUI sets@{ub} run over the same player,
parsers, playback settings, and status/control protocol:

  - @{b}MintVID@{ub}, iptvgui, ytgui use ReAction V44.
  - @{b}MintVID-GT@{ub}, iptvgui-GT, ytgui-GT use only GadTools/Intuition
    V37, for a standard AmigaOS 3.0 installation. Start MintVID-GT; it
    opens the matching -GT browsers automatically.

Keep one complete GUI set beside mrplay (or put mrplay on the command
path). Both editions drive the same underlying mrplay process and the
same options, so behaviour matches between them - only the front end
differs.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node IPTVBrowser "IPTV browser"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}Live channel directory@{ub}

Both controllers include an IPTV... launcher for their matching
directory window. It reads valid cached channels.json and streams.json
from PROGDIR:Cache/IPTV/ immediately; the default public directory is
iptv-org (channels.json, streams.json, countries.json, categories.json).

MintVID does not host or redistribute television channels: iptv-org is a
collection of publicly available links, and individual links may be
offline, geo-blocked, or require request headers.

Cached JSON is used immediately, refreshed after 24 hours, and replaced
only after a complete download parses successfully - a failed refresh
leaves the prior cache intact. Manual HTTP/HTTPS media URLs, M3U8
playlists, and simple #EXTM3U lists use the normal MintVID URL/player
pipeline; playback still depends on the existing demuxers and codecs.
HLS prefers supported low-resolution variants (640 px wide by default)
and cannot make DRM, login-only, unsupported-codec, or dead streams
playable.

Classic 68040/060 H.264 results depend heavily on the exact stream and output
mode. With the 1.2.0 optimisations, the lowest-resolution BBC One HLS stream
has approached real time on a tested real 68060 using AGA/HAM8, while higher
resolutions remain beyond normal classic hardware. PiStorm/Emu68 remains the
practical tier for broader H.264 IPTV playback.

Only the selected country's channels are held in RAM; unrelated global
streams are validated and discarded. Each channel retains up to four
preferred stream URLs, and the window's Next Stream button advances
through them without silently looping. Double-clicking a channel plays
it directly, without a separate Play press.

IPTV playback inherits a snapshot of the ReAction controller's display,
C2P, lace, and 2x selections when IPTV... is pressed; close and reopen
the window after changing controller settings to pick up new ones. A
Shell-launched iptvgui uses safe AGA/Standard, lace-off, 2x-off,
low-bandwidth HLS defaults.

Building with SSL=1 enables the iptv-org directory download over HTTPS;
without it, the browser stays usable for cached data and manual URLs,
and a refresh explicitly reports that it needs rebuilding with SSL=1.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node YouTube "YouTube"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}Native YouTube playback@{ub}

mrplay can resolve a public YouTube Live watch/share URL natively. It
fetches the watch page with a browser user agent, extracts the signed
HLS manifest URL, validates it, and hands it to the normal HLS
variant/segment pipeline. Resolution happens on the Amiga itself, so
IP-bound signed URLs are not borrowed from a remote service.

For ordinary uploads, the resolver also tries YouTube's muxed 360p MP4
and, where still supplied, muxed 720p MP4 - both contain H.264 video and
AAC audio together, so MintVID's existing seekable HTTP/MP4 path plays
them without downloading or merging separate streams. Only a direct
signed HTTPS Google Video URL is accepted; ciphered URLs and unresolved
player challenges are rejected. Selecting 720p, 1080p, or Best tries
720p first and falls back to 360p automatically; Low, 360p, and 480p
stay on the 360p muxed format. There is no standard muxed 480p or 1080p
target yet.

On classic 68040/060 machines H.264 remains highly dependent on the stream and
output mode. The lowest-resolution BBC One HLS stream has approached real time
on a tested real 68060 with AGA/HAM8 after the 1.2.0 work, but ordinary 360p
uploads and higher resolutions remain beyond normal classic hardware.
PiStorm/Emu68 is the practical target for the broader YouTube range.

This support is intentionally narrow: age/login/region-restricted
videos, DRM, uploads without a usable muxed format, and YouTube's
private-schema changes can all produce a clean unsupported error rather
than a crash.

@{b}Searching@{ub}

The ReAction controller's YouTube... button opens the separate ytgui
search window. It searches YouTube's public results page without an API
key, shows the title and channel, and starts the selected result through
the same native resolver. The Quality button cycles through Low, 360p,
480p, 720p, 1080p, and unrestricted Best. The search-type selector
defaults to Live and also offers All, Videos, and Shorts. Selecting a
result and pressing Channel videos follows its channel ID to that
channel's public uploads list.

The transport row controls the separate player process directly: Play
first cleanly replaces the current video, Pause and Fast toggle their
modes, Vol -/+ adjusts Paula in steps, Fullscreen toggles the RTG
window, and Stop exits the player. Double-clicking a result plays it
directly, without a separate Play press.

Build with SSL=1 and keep ytgui beside MintVID and mrplay.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node Formats "Supported formats"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}Containers@{ub}: AVI, QuickTime MOV/MP4, Matroska/MKV, MPEG-TS/M2TS,
raw MPEG-1 program streams, raw MJPEG/MPEG-4 Visual streams, and direct
HTTP(S)/HLS URLs (including live IPTV playlists and YouTube).

@{b}Video codecs@{ub}: Cinepak, Microsoft Video 1, Microsoft RLE8,
uncompressed UYVY422, H.263 baseline, Motion-JPEG, MPEG-1, MPEG-2 (with
B-frames), MPEG-4 Part 2 (ISO ASP and Microsoft MP42/DIV2), Windows Media
Video 7 (WMV1), Windows Media Video 8 (WMV2), and H.264/AVC Baseline/Main/
High Profile.

WMV1's low-bitrate spatial intra/inter prediction mode is deliberately
rejected rather than approximated. WMV2 IntraX8 (J-frame) coding is also not
supported. Normal tested WMV1/WMV2 AVI I/P streams use the native decoders.

@{b}Audio to Paula@{ub}: PCM, MP2, MP3, AAC-LC (raw/ADTS/LATM), and
fixed-point AC-3 (stereo downmix). MP3/AAC decoding reuses the
MintAMP/Helix code.

MintVID's core decoders are regression-tested against ffmpeg using the
project's test fixtures where applicable. The portable core is also
cross-built and exercised on a big-endian m68k target under QEMU to catch
endianness and alignment problems that a little-endian x86 host build can
miss.

MintVID uses a modular decoder architecture rather than being tied to one
fixed format, so the supported codec list can continue to grow. It is not a
video editor or encoder: use ffmpeg or similar on a modern machine to prepare
source material, then play the result on the Amiga.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node Troubleshooting "Troubleshooting"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}"Could not load iptvgui / ytgui / mrplay"@{ub}

Keep the whole release drawer together - MintVID, MintVID-GT, mrplay,
iptvgui, ytgui and the -GT set all expect to find each other
PROGDIR:-relative, in the same directory they shipped in.

@{b}Stack overflows / crashes under libavc (H.264)@{ub}

mrplay carries a $STACK:320000 AmigaOS stack cookie because libavc needs
substantially more stack than the classic Shell default. On systems that
don't honour stack cookies, run "Stack 320000" before starting the
player.

@{b}HTTPS/YouTube/IPTV refresh says it needs SSL=1@{ub}

That build was compiled without AmiSSL support. Rebuild with SSL=1 (and
SSLCERTS=1 to verify server certificates), or use cached IPTV data and
plain HTTP URLs in the meantime.

@{b}A stream won't play@{ub}

MintVID cannot make DRM, login-only, geo-blocked, or unsupported-codec
streams playable - that is a property of the source, not a bug to
report. Direct AVI/MOV/MP4 URLs also need a finite, byte-addressable
resource: the server must supply Content-Length or Content-Range and
honour byte ranges when the container seeks.

If none of the above explains what you're seeing, see
@{" Reporting a problem " LINK ReportingProblems}.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node ReportingProblems "Reporting a problem"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}Reporting a problem@{ub}

Please open an issue on the project's GitHub page and include:

  1. The exact command line or GUI steps used to reproduce it (media
     path/URL, display mode, C2P chooser setting, laced/2x, H.264 speed
     mode), and which release drawer (MintVID030/040/060) and CPU/board
     you ran it on - PiStorm/Emu68, Vampire/Apollo, or real 68k silicon.

  2. mrplay's own console output from that run. On launch it already
     reports the container type, video codec/FourCC, dimensions, frame
     rate and audio format - re-run with @{i}--time@{ui} added if the
     problem looks performance-related; that also prints which display/
     C2P implementation actually ran (for example @{i}kalms-040@{ui},
     @{i}kalms-2x2@{ui}, @{i}wpa@{ui}) and a per-stage timing breakdown.

  3. For a crash: the exact AmigaOS "Software Failure" text (task name
     and error number), and whether it happened on real hardware,
     PiStorm/Emu68, or an emulator such as WinUAE.

  4. For a YouTube/IPTV problem: the public video/channel URL or IPTV
     directory entry, since MintVID's YouTube/HLS resolution behaviour is
     often specific to one video, channel, or region.

MintVID's codecs are regression-tested against ffmpeg on the host and
cross-built for real big-endian m68k under QEMU (see CLAUDE.md in the
source tree) before anything ships, but the Amiga-specific display,
audio and GUI layers can only be verified on real hardware or PiStorm -
so a clear, reproducible report from actual hardware is genuinely the
fastest path to a fix.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

@node Licensing "Licensing"
@toc MintVID.guide/MAIN
@{b}MintVID's own code@{ub} (the portable core, the Amiga display/audio
layers, and every GUI frontend) is MIT-licensed and available from its
project repository.

It links several dependencies that keep their own licences, and
distributing a @{i}built binary@{ui} means complying with all of them at
once, not just MIT:

  - libmpeg2 (VideoLAN) and the fixed-point Rockbox/a52dec AC-3 core are
    GPL-2.0-or-later, and are statically linked into mrplay. That makes
    the compiled binary a combined work under GPL-2.0-or-later -
    redistributing binaries obliges you to also offer corresponding
    source, per the GPL, even though MintVID's own contribution is MIT.
  - Ittiam libavc (H.264) is Apache-2.0.
  - MintAMP/Helix is a separately licensed submodule; retain its
    notices. In particular the Helix AAC decoder path it pulls in is
    licensed under RealNetworks' RPSL, not GPL.

This player was inspired by RiVA 0.54 (Fellner/Toeroek/Richter), the
fastest 68k MPEG-1 player, previously distributed on Aminet - but is a
fresh, unrelated codebase and does not include or depend on RiVA's
source.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MintVID.guide/MAIN 0}
@endnode

